<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philippeville massacre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Philippeville massacre took place on 20 August 1955 in and around Philippeville, now Skikda, in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. FLN fighters and armed villagers attacked Europeans and pro-French Muslims, killing over a hundred, including women and children at the mining village of El Halia. French reprisals killed thousands of Muslim Algerians, deepening the divide between the communities and escalating the war.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Algerian War had begun on 1 November 1954. By the summer of 1955 French counterinsurgency had left the FLN in a weak position: only Wilaya II, one of its sections, could mount an offensive, popular support was low, and many of the Algerian Muslim elite favoured a settlement with the French government. Youcef Zighoud, the leader of Wilaya II, planned an attack on pied-noir civilians in the hope that harsh French retaliation would break the bond between the communities, raise support for the rebellion and end any chance of a conciliatory settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 20 August 1955 a few hundred FLN members led crowds of several thousand local Muslim civilians against settlements of the Constantine region, spreading false rumours of an imminent Egyptian landing. At Philippeville the mob killed Europeans in the streets, but police and paratroopers defeated the assault; 14 police officers were killed and the bodies of over 100 insurgents were found afterwards. At the El-Halia pyrite mine, where about 130 Europeans and 2,000 Muslims lived, 37 Europeans were murdered, mostly women and children. An assault on the military outpost near El Khroub was repulsed, and in Constantine commandos killed Allouah Abbas, a moderate politician and nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferhat-abbas">Ferhat Abbas</a>.</p><p>French reprisals followed. Paratroopers shot about 80 Algerian men found at El-Halia, 60 captured insurgents were shot at El Khroub, and suspected villages were razed by the French Air Force. In Philippeville the stadium became an interrogation centre where men were executed without trial; Robert Lambotte's photograph of the bodies, published in L'Humanité, caused outrage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Vigilante militias, armed by the city's mayor, Paul-Dominique Benquet-Crevaux, killed dozens more.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The official count was 71 European civilians, 21 Algerian civilians and 31 law enforcement officers killed by the insurgents; the historian Roger Vétillard gave 117, 42 and 47. The toll of the reprisals is disputed: 1,273 officially, between 3,000 and 5,000 according to Charles-Robert Ageron, 12,000 according to the FLN. The events opened a lasting divide between the communities. Abane Ramdane and Larbi Ben M'hidi criticised Zighoud for sending barely armed civilians to their deaths, 61 moderate Muslim politicians condemned the repression, and Governor Jacques Soustelle abandoned conciliation. The number of FLN fighters in the region tripled by the end of 1955.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philippeville massacre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war escalated after the <b>Philippeville massacre</b> of August 1955, and in 1957 paratroopers under General Jacques Massu broke the FLN network in the Battle of Algiers through the systematic use of torture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
